  "body": "From https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#case-sensitivity-and-string-comparison : \n\n> Comparing two strings in an ASCII case-insensitive manner means comparing them exactly, code point for code point, except that the characters in the range U+0041 to U+005A (i.e. LATIN CAPITAL LETTER A to LATIN CAPITAL LETTER Z) and the corresponding characters in the range U+0061 to U+007A (i.e. LATIN SMALL LETTER A to LATIN SMALL LETTER Z) are considered to also match.\n\nThis comparison will result in different answers for strings that are in NFD vs NFC normalization form, even if both are normalized to the same form. The NFD `\"o\\u0308\"` (combining umlaut) would be ASCII case-insensitive equal to  `\"O\\u0308\"`, but the pre-combined forms in NFC `\"\\u00F6\"` and `\"\\u00D6\"` (pre-combined o/O with umlaut).\n\nAlgorithms defined by the Unicode Consortium, such as case conversion and collation, are defined so as to be invariant under normalization forms NFD/NFC (though maybe not NFKD/NFKC). This algorithm is not. It could lead to strange results when used with languages that tend to use non-combined characters.\n\nAs a possible fix (though this may have some performance cost), non-equal ASCII characters which are followed by combining marks could be considered case-insensitive unequal. Or, you could say that any two non-equal strings which contain a non-ASCII character are case-insensitive unequal.\n\ncc @jungshik\n",
